        <Options>
            <Main applyHouseholdOperatingConditions="true" applyReducedOperatingConditions="false" atypicalElectricalLoads="false" waterConservation="true" referenceHouse="false">
                <Vermiculite code="3">
                    <English>No Vermiculite</English>
                    <French>Pas de Vermiculite</French>
                </Vermiculite>
            </Main>
            <HouseholdOperatingConditions numberOfOccupants="2" coolingSeasonMonths="0" isClothesWasherEnergySaver="true" isLightingEnergySaver="true" areAppliancesEnergySaver="true">
                <Thermostat heatingDaytime="22" heatingNighttime="16" cooling="25" />
            </HouseholdOperatingConditions>
            <WaterConservation LowFlushToilets="3" LowFlushToiletsUpgrade="3" />
            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

Overall, your house has a lot of excess air leakage, however there are some key areas where air leakage occurs. Air leakage was observed through electrical outlets on your exterior walls, the weather stripping along the exterior doors, and along the roof connection to the main floor walls of the original structure of the home. 

I recommend to outfit electrical outlets located along the exterior walls with an electrical wall plate gasket. 

I recommend replacing the weather stripping along all of your exterior doors. There was observed air leakage from both doors in the entrance area, which is potentially the reason why this area is typically cold. The basement door (that leads to the backyard) appears to make poor contact with the framing. Using a screw in aluminum style weather stripping may help to create a better seal, otherwise this door/frame may need to be adjusted/replaced. 

As you mentioned a new roof is potentially being conisdered in the near future, this will help eliminate the poor connection along the original framed roof. These air leakage locations were very observable in the corners of the upstairs front bedroom, and along the connection with the porch. 

Air leakage was also observed through the bathroom exhaust fans located on the main and upper floors. I recommend you replace your exhaust fans with an ENERGY STARÂ® rated model, which will have a new damper built into the new unit.



Savings: For a minimum 10% improvement you could potentially save $ 320 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 9.5 GJ, and qualify for up to$ 500 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must have a home energy evaluation, which includes an air leakage test, before and after your retrofits. For a full rebate, the rebate application, with a copy of the first page of both home energy evaluation reports, must be received within three months of the second energy evaluation. For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

The exterior walls of your house appear to be a mix of 2x6 studs with 6 inches (R20) of fiberglass batt insulation, as well as 2x3 (R8), and 2x4 studs with 4 inches (R12) of fiberglass batt insulation, as well as 2x3 (R8). I have modelled adding 2 inches (R10) of extruded polystyrene insulation to all exterior walls of the home.



Savings:

By adding R10 of extruded polystyrene insulation, you could potentially save $ 540 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 15.7 GJ, and qualify for up to$ 1,875 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 0.7 (R-3.8). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website:

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program</mainWalls>
                <ceilings />
                <ceilingsCathedral>

The cathedral ceiling of your house appears to have 8 inches (R28) fiberglass batt insulation. As you mentioned a new roof is potentially planned in the near future, I have modelled a 2x12 structure with 11.0 inches (R40) of insulation.



Savings:

By increasing the insulation to 11.0 inches (R40) with the new roof, you could potentially save $ 290 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 8.5 GJ.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total ceiling area and achieve a minimum insulation value of RSI 7.0 (R-40). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of ceiling area that is insulated compared to the total ceiling area after the retrofits. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

</ceilingsCathedral>
                <foundation>

Your basement walls (the foundation of the original home, which contains the mechanical room, as well as suite kitchen &amp; bathroom) appears to be 8 inches of concrete with no insulation. As the space is fairly restricted in the mechnical room for adding insulation, I have modeled adding 2 inches (R10) rigid foam, extruded polystyrene, insulation to the concrete walls. 



Savings:

By adding 2 inches (R10) rigid foam extruded polystyrene insulation to the concrete walls, you could potentially save $ 1,000 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 30.5 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 625 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total foundation wall area including crawlspaces (when applicable), and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.8 (R-10). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

</foundation>
                <HotWaterSystem>

You currently have a 189 litre electric hot water tank, which is also indirectly heated by a loop from the boiler. Although electric hot water tanks are efficient from the perspective that 100% of the electricity used either heats the water inside or the heat radiating from the tank supplements the heating of the home, electricity is very costly.

As you currently have an efficient oil boiler, it would be ideal to use your boiler to heat your hot water year round. Winter is assumed to be 9 months of the year, and the remaining 3 months is typically when the boiler would be shut down. 



Savings:

By using your current oil fired boiler to heat your hot water all year, you could potentially save $ 230 per year in energy costs. 



Upgraded Boiler Savings:

By upgrading your boiler to a 95% condensing propane fired boiler (and continuing to heat your hot water using the new boiler), you could potentially save an additional $ 190 per year in energy costs. </HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

BOILER

Your current oil fired boiler was originally rated at 85% AFUE. When it is time to replace it, I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STARÂ® qualified high-efficiency condensing propane boiler with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE. If you choose a high-efficiency condensing propane boiler, the current chimney can be eliminated altogether.



Savings:

By replacing your heating system with a high-efficiency condensing propane boiler with an efficiency of 95% AFUE, you could potentially save $ 2,050 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 44.9 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 750 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



DOMESTIC HOT WATER

You currently have a 189 litre electric hot water tank, which is also indirectly heated by a loop from the boiler. Although electric hot water tanks are efficient from the perspective that 100% of the electricity used either heats the water inside or the heat radiating from the tank supplements the heating of the home, electricity is very costly.

As you currently have an efficient oil boiler, it would be ideal to use your boiler to heat your hot water year round. Winter is assumed to be 9 months of the year, and the remaining 3 months is typically when the boiler would be shut down. 



Savings:

By using your current oil fired boiler to heat your hot water all year, you could potentially save $ 230 per year in energy costs. 



Upgraded Boiler Savings:

By upgrading your boiler to a 95% condensing propane fired boiler (and continuing to heat your hot water using the new boiler), you could potentially save an additional $ 190 per year in energy costs. </HeatingSystem>
                <AdditionalComments>COMBUSTION AIR

I did not see a combustion air supply pipe to provide combustion air to the boiler and hot water tank.

I suggest you have a qualified heating contractor investigate this further and ensure both the fresh air supply requirements of your house and the combustion air supply needs of your boiler and hot water tank are satisfied. The combustion air pipe should have an electric damper installed to ensure combustion air will only be introduced into the room when the boiler or hot water tank is operating.

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



WOODSTOVE

Savings (with current oil boiler):

By using your current wood stove for 50% of your heating load, you could potentially save $ 2,100 per year in energy savings. This assumes a cost of $ 100 per cord of wood for harvesting your own. 

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



HIGH VELOCITY - FAN COIL

I recommend to have someone look at the High Velocity fan coil installed. During the time of the assesment, the unit appeared to be running constantly (always on), and does not appear to have a control panel connected (the control wires appear to be spliced together, which would explain why it is constantly running). Your fan coil unit also has a fresh air supply pipe connected to the return air plenum and there is an internal damper that you can turn to control the anmount of fresh air entering your home. A good indicator for how much this should be opened is the level of humidity in the house. Too much humidity means you open it further, and too dry inside means you can close it further.



As this unit is constantly running (and bringing in fresh air), you may be over ventilating your home and bringing in more fresh air than is required, therefore increasing the heating load. If possible, I suggest a control system be installed, in which it will recognize when there is a demand for heat from the boiler, but also have the option to turn the fan on manually. As the intake is located centrally by the stairwell, this is a great option for circulating heat around the home while the pellet stove is running. 

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



FUEL COSTS

The following values have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Yellowknife:

Electricity - $17.81 min, $0.32 per kWh

Oil  - $1.29 per litre 

Propane - $0.80 per litre

Wood - $341.25 per cord 

Pellets - $5.51 per bag

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



DISCLAIMER

The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building.  This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



YOUR ENERGY ADVISOR

Please do not hesitate to contact me if you have any questions.



Sincerely,



&lt;b&gt;Michael Goodwin

Energy Management Specialist&lt;/b&gt;

Arctic Energy Alliance

Phone: (867) 920-3333

Fax: (867) 873-0303

E-mail: michael.goodwin@aea.nt.ca



Arctic Energy Alliance: Taking action on high heating and power bills, high transportation costs, and climate change!

</AdditionalComments>
            </RURComments>
